## Steps and evidence

### STEP 1 -- "set the mirror direction to both" and read it back
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#interface port1.0.10
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ(config-if)#mirror interface port1.0.48 direction both
x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: both
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ#show mirror interface port1.0.48
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.10
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: both
  Monitored Port Name: port1.0.48
x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.10
   mirror interface port1.0.48 direction both
  => STEP 1 PASS: the CLI accepts `direction both` and both show forms and the running-config
     report it. The CONFIGURATION half of the case is correct.

### STEP 2 -- frame-level proof that `both` mirrors both directions         FAIL
Expected: the 6 echo REQUESTS the DUT receives on port1.0.48 AND the 6 echo REPLIES it
transmits out of it -- 12 frames. Measured, after the destination port was bounced:
host# ./cap.sh both          (cap-both.txt 1 B, cap-both.err)
  0 packets captured / 0 packets received by filter / 0 packets dropped by kernel
  ping: 6 transmitted, 6 received, 0% packet loss
  counted: echo REQUESTS = 0 ; echo REPLIES = 0
  => STEP 2 FAIL: nothing at all was copied, while the identical flow passed end to end.

### STEP 3 -- everything tried to rule out method, before grading
  a) re-measure without changing anything (settle time)
     cap-both-retry.txt 1 B  -> 0 requests, 0 replies
  b) a SECOND destination-port bounce, 25 s settle
     cap-both-bounce2.txt 1 B -> 0 requests, 0 replies
  c) port and host state during the failure
     x960-56YSQ#show interface port1.0.10 status
       port1.0.10  -  connected  1  a-full a-1000  10GBASE-TM        <-- link up
     host# ip -br link show dev eth3
       eth3 UP 00:0d:b9:55:77:0e <BROADCAST,MULTICAST,UP,LOWER_UP>   <-- host NIC up
     tcpdump reported "0 packets RECEIVED BY FILTER" -- nothing reached eth3 at all, so this
     is not a filter, decode or promiscuity problem.
  d) IS THE MIRROR ENGINE WEDGED? Re-applied `direction transmit`, clean + bounce, same session:
     cap-tx-control-after-both.txt 541 B -> 6 echo REPLIES, 0 requests      WORKS
     So the engine is NOT wedged and the measurement method is sound.
  e) clean re-apply of `both` to confirm reproducibility
     cap-both-confirm.txt 1 B -> 0 requests, 0 replies
  => STEP 3 done: the failure is specific to `direction both`, reproducible, and not explained
     by link state, host capture, settle time, or the M1 re-application defect.

