## Defect M3 -- `mirror interface <src> direction both` copies NO frames
Summary of the discriminating evidence, all on one boot, one cabling, one 6-ping flow
(a 1-byte capture file means nothing arrived; ~535-547 B means frames arrived):

  direction   capture file                  size   requests  replies  verdict
  transmit    cap-tx-sameboot.txt           535 B      0        6     works
  receive     cap-rx-sameboot.txt           547 B      6        0     works
  both        cap-both-afterboot.txt          1 B      0        0     NOTHING COPIED

`show mirror`, `show mirror interface port1.0.48` and the running-config all report the mirror
as Enabled with direction both and the correct monitored port throughout. There is no error,
no log message and no CLI indication that anything is wrong -- only a frame capture reveals it.
Taken with M2 from T44968, the pattern is that this platform's mirror status output cannot be
relied on to describe what the dataplane is doing.
Reproduced 5 times: cap-both.txt, cap-both-retry.txt, cap-both-bounce2.txt, cap-both-confirm.txt,
cap-both-afterboot.txt -- all 1 byte.
CR wording is the Test Engineer's call; these are the measurements.

FAIL CONDITION: with `mirror interface port1.0.48 direction both` configured on the
destination port, a 6-ping flow across the monitored port must deliver BOTH directions to the
mirror destination -- 6 echo requests (what the DUT receives on port1.0.48) AND 6 echo replies
(what it transmits out of it), 12 frames in total.
  expected : 6 echo requests AND 6 echo replies at tb277 eth3
  observed : 0 echo requests and 0 echo replies, five separate runs, every capture file 1 byte
             (cap-both.txt, cap-both-retry.txt, cap-both-bounce2.txt, cap-both-confirm.txt,
              cap-both-afterboot.txt), tcpdump reporting "0 packets received by filter"
  the ping passed 6/6 at 0% loss in every one of those runs, so the traffic to be mirrored
  was present on the monitored port throughout.
WHY THIS IS THE PRODUCT, NOT THE METHOD: `direction transmit` and `direction receive` were
applied on the SAME boot, the SAME cabling, the SAME 6-ping flow and by the SAME procedure
(remove -> apply -> bounce the destination -> measure), and each delivered exactly its expected
frames -- transmit 6 replies and 0 requests (cap-tx-sameboot.txt, 535 B), receive 6 requests and
0 replies (cap-rx-sameboot.txt, 547 B). A method, bench or capture fault would have taken those
two down with it. The decisive run was on a PRISTINE BOOT: `both` was saved to startup-config
and the DUT reloaded, so the hardware was programmed from flash with NO re-application history,
which is what rules out defect M1 as the explanation. It still copied nothing.

TRIED: everything below was done before grading, and none of it changed the result.
  - re-measured with no configuration change, in case the mirror needed settle time
    -> cap-both-retry.txt, 1 byte
  - bounced the destination port a SECOND time with 25 s settle
    -> cap-both-bounce2.txt, 1 byte
  - checked the link at both ends during the failure: `show interface port1.0.10 status` read
    `connected ... a-full a-1000`, and the host NIC read `UP ... LOWER_UP`
  - checked it was not a capture-side fault: tcpdump reported "0 packets RECEIVED BY FILTER",
    so nothing reached eth3 at all, and an earlier UNFILTERED capture in this group caught not
    one frame of any kind in 14 s
  - checked the mirror engine was not wedged by the preceding reconfigurations: re-applied
    `direction transmit` mid-session, clean + bounce -> cap-tx-control-after-both.txt, 541 B,
    6 echo replies. The engine was working seconds before and after `both` failed.
  - saved and reloaded, then re-tested on the pristine boot, and re-tested both working
    directions on that same boot

VERDICT: FAIL -- `mirror interface port1.0.48 direction both` is accepted and reported correctly
by every CLI status command, and survives a save and reload, but copies NO frames to the mirror
destination. `direction transmit` and `direction receive` both work on the same boot, same
cabling and same traffic, so the platform's mirroring and the test method are sound and the
fault is specific to `both`. Reproduced five times including on a pristine boot with no
reconfiguration history.
